Rem ==================================================================
Rem simplex.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: linear programming
Rem
Rem The public surface:
Rem
Rem   Problem(Costs, A, Rhs)      minimize Costs . x
Rem                               s.t. A x >= Rhs,  x >= 0
Rem   Optimize(p As Problem) As Solution
Rem
Rem   SpProblem(Costs, SA, Rhs)   the same problem with a Sparse
Rem                               constraint matrix, for big thin ones
Rem   SpOptimize(p As SpProblem) As Solution
Rem
Rem   Solution: Status ("OPTIMAL" | "UNBOUNDED" | "MAXITER"),
Rem             X (the primal variables), Cost,
Rem             DualPrices (shadow price per constraint — the change in
Rem             Cost per unit of Rhs; the sensitivity analysis),
Rem             Reduced (reduced cost per variable), Iterations.
Rem
Rem Revised simplex over the Sparse machine, solved via the dual
Rem (max Rhs.y s.t. Transp(A) y <= Costs): at optimality the simplex
Rem multipliers are exactly the primal x. Negative objective
Rem coefficients make the dual's slack start infeasible, so those rows
Rem are negated and given big-M artificial variables (when every cost
Rem is >= 0 no artificials are created and the computation is exactly
Rem the classic slack start). Honest statuses: "INFEASIBLE" when the
Rem dual is unbounded (the primal has no feasible point) and
Rem "UNBOUNDED-OR-INFEASIBLE" when artificials cannot be driven out
Rem (the dual is infeasible). Pure throughout; effects stay with the
Rem caller.
Rem
Rem There is one solver. Optimize is SpOptimize with the dense matrix
Rem crossed over at the door, and both walk the same pivots: pricing
Rem skips a stored zero that a dense scan would have multiplied by
Rem nothing and added, so the two agree to the last bit, iteration
Rem count included.
Rem
Rem What is sparse and what is not. The dual matrix is sparse: its
Rem slack and artificial columns are single entries, never a
Rem materialised identity, and pricing walks the stored entries of a
Rem column instead of every cell of one. The basis inverse is a dense
Rem n x n Matrix updated in place-of-a-kind by an eta step, n being the
Rem number of PRIMAL VARIABLES — so that, and not the constraint count,
Rem is the ceiling: this is a solver for hundreds of variables with as
Rem many constraints as you like. Product-form inverses and sparse LU
Rem are what a thousand variables would want, and both fit behind these
Rem same words if that day comes.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "matrix.shoddy"
Include "sparse.shoddy"

Type Problem
    Costs As Array Of Number
    A     As Matrix
    Rhs   As Array Of Number

Rem The same statement of the same problem, holding its constraints
Rem sparsely. A Problem crosses to one of these at the door of
Rem Optimize; a caller with a big thin matrix — an MPS file, a branch
Rem and bound node — builds one directly and never densifies at all.

Type SpProblem
    Costs As Array Of Number
    SA    As Sparse
    Rhs   As Array Of Number

Type Solution
    Status     As String
    X          As Array Of Number
    Cost       As Number
    DualPrices As Array Of Number
    Reduced    As Array Of Number
    Iterations As Number

Rem The dual in standard form (internal plumbing). NStruct = number of
Rem structural dual variables (primal constraints); Flip records which
Rem dual rows were negated for the big-M start (multiplier signs).

Type Dual
    DA      As Sparse
    DCost   As Array Of Number
    DRhs    As Array Of Number
    NStruct As Number
    Flip    As Array Of Number

Rem ---- small helpers ---------------------------------------------------

Def SignFor(v As Number) As Number
    If v < 0 Then
        -1
    Else
        1

Def MaxAbs(xs As Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(xs, 0, Fn(a, v) => Max(a, Abs(v)))

Def PosOf(xs As List, v As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If Nth(xs, k) = v Then
        k
    Else
        PosOf(xs, v, k + 1)

Def BasisSlot(i As Number, flip As Array Of Number, m As Number, n As Number, arts As List) As Number
    If Nth(flip, i) > 0 Then
        m + i
    Else
        m + n + PosOf(arts, i, 1)

Rem Which columns are in the basis, as a flag per column. Pricing asks
Rem this of every column on every pass, and asking a flat array is a
Rem lookup where searching the basis for the column's number was a scan
Rem of the whole basis - and a fresh list to scan - per column.

Def BasisFlags(basis As Array Of Number, ncols As Number) As Array Of Number
    Fold(basis, Dim(ncols, 0), Fn(a, j) => SetNth(a, j, 1))

Rem ---- pricing: entering column (most positive reduced cost) --------

Def PickEnter(j As Number, bestJ As Number, bestR As Number, inb As Array Of Number, y As Array Of Number, du As Dual) As Number
    If j > Length(DCost(du)) Then
        bestJ
    Else
        If Nth(inb, j) > 0 Then
            PickEnter(j + 1, bestJ, bestR, inb, y, du)
        Else
            Let r = Nth(DCost(du), j) - SpColDot(DA(du), j, y)
            If r > bestR + 0.000001 Then
                PickEnter(j + 1, j, r, inb, y, du)
            Else
                PickEnter(j + 1, bestJ, bestR, inb, y, du)

Rem ---- ratio test: leaving row (0 means unbounded) -------------------

Def PickLeave(i As Number, bestI As Number, bestRatio As Number, xb As Array Of Number, d As Array Of Number) As Number
    If i > Length(d) Then
        bestI
    Else
        If Nth(d, i) > 0.000001 Then
            Let ratio = Nth(xb, i) / Nth(d, i)
            If ratio < bestRatio Then
                PickLeave(i + 1, i, ratio, xb, d)
            Else
                PickLeave(i + 1, bestI, bestRatio, xb, d)
        Else
            PickLeave(i + 1, bestI, bestRatio, xb, d)

Rem ---- the two solves against the basis inverse ------------------------
Rem BTran answers y = Transp(binv) . cb without building the transpose:
Rem column i of binv, down the rows, is row i of its transpose.

Def BTranAt(binv As Matrix, i As Number, cb As Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(Range(1, Length(cb)), 0, Fn(s, k) => s + MatGet(binv, k, i) * Nth(cb, k))

Def BTran(binv As Matrix, cb As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, Cols(binv)), Fn(i) => BTranAt(binv, i, cb)))

Rem FTran answers d = binv . a_e, reaching only the columns of binv the
Rem entering column actually has an entry in: o(n . nnz(a_e)) where the
Rem dense product was o(n squared), and the same sum in the same order,
Rem because the terms left out were the ones worth nothing.

Def FTranAt(binv As Matrix, i As Number, ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, k As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If k > Length(ix) Then
        acc
    Else
        FTranAt(binv, i, ix, vs, k + 1, acc + MatGet(binv, i, Nth(ix, k)) * Nth(vs, k))

Def FTran(binv As Matrix, ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, Rows(binv)), Fn(i) => FTranAt(binv, i, ix, vs, 1, 0)))

Rem ---- eta-style basis inverse update ---------------------------------

Def UpdRow(i As Number, r As Number, binv As Matrix, d As Array Of Number, pivRow As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    If i = r Then
        pivRow
    Else
        VSub(MatRow(binv, i), VScale(pivRow, Nth(d, i)))

Def UpdateBinv(binv As Matrix, d As Array Of Number, r As Number) As Matrix
    Let pivRow = VScale(MatRow(binv, r), 1 / Nth(d, r))
    MatFromRows(Map(Range(1, Rows(binv)), Fn(i) => UpdRow(i, r, binv, d, pivRow)))

Rem ---- the basic solution ----------------------------------------------
Rem xb = binv . DRhs, read straight out of the inverse rather than
Rem through MatVec, which would cut a fresh row array loose on every
Rem row of every iteration to dot it once and drop it.
Rem
Rem Riding the eta step instead - updating xb alongside binv, the way
Rem the inverse itself is updated - was tried and put back. It walks
Rem the same 117 pivots on BLEND and answers the same X, but Cost and
Rem the duals come out a bit or two adrift, because it accumulates its
Rem own rounding across the pivots where this re-reads the inverse. An
Rem explicit inverse is kept here anyway; reading it is both the exact
Rem answer and the same o(n squared) the inverse update already costs.

Def BasisSolAt(binv As Matrix, i As Number, rhs As Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(Range(1, Length(rhs)), 0, Fn(s, k) => s + MatGet(binv, i, k) * Nth(rhs, k))

Def BasisSol(binv As Matrix, rhs As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, Rows(binv)), Fn(i) => BasisSolAt(binv, i, rhs)))

Rem ---- reading the optimal basis: the value of dual variable k --------

Def BasisVal(k As Number, i As Number, basis As Array Of Number, xb As Array Of Number) As Number
    If i > Length(basis) Then
        0
    Else
        If Nth(basis, i) = k Then
            Nth(xb, i)
        Else
            BasisVal(k, i + 1, basis, xb)

Rem ---- the revised simplex loop: pure, returns a Solution -------------
Rem At optimality the dual's structural variables (1..m) are the primal
Rem constraints' shadow prices; its slacks (m+1..m+n) are the primal
Rem variables' reduced costs.

Def Solve(basis As Array Of Number, inb As Array Of Number, binv As Matrix, iter As Number, du As Dual) As Solution
    If iter > 100000 Then
        Solution("MAXITER", Dim(0, 0), 0, Dim(0, 0), Dim(0, 0), iter)
    Else
        Let cb = Map(basis, Fn(j) => Nth(DCost(du), j))
        Let y = BTran(binv, cb)
        Let xb = BasisSol(binv, DRhs(du))
        Let e = PickEnter(1, 0, 0, inb, y, du)
        If e = 0 Then
            Let n = Length(DRhs(du))
            Let m = NStruct(du)
            Let aSum = Fold(Range(m + n + 1, Length(DCost(du))), 0, Fn(a, k) => a + BasisVal(k, 1, basis, xb))
            If aSum > 0.000001 Then
                Rem artificials could not be driven out: the dual is
                Rem infeasible, so the primal is unbounded or empty
                Solution("UNBOUNDED-OR-INFEASIBLE", Dim(0, 0), 0, Dim(0, 0), Dim(0, 0), iter)
            Else
                Let duals = ToArray(Map(Range(1, m), Fn(k) => BasisVal(k, 1, basis, xb)))
                Let red = ToArray(Map(Range(m + 1, m + n), Fn(k) => BasisVal(k, 1, basis, xb)))
                Let px = ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Nth(y, i) * Nth(Flip(du), i)))
                Solution(Status = "OPTIMAL", X = px, Cost = Dot(cb, xb), DualPrices = duals, Reduced = red, Iterations = iter)
        Else
            Let d = FTran(binv, SpColIx(DA(du), e), SpColVs(DA(du), e))
            Let r = PickLeave(1, 0, 999999999, xb, d)
            If r = 0 Then
                Rem the dual is unbounded, so the primal has no feasible point
                Solution("INFEASIBLE", Dim(0, 0), 0, Dim(0, 0), Dim(0, 0), iter)
            Else
                Solve(SetNth(basis, r, e), SetNth(SetNth(inb, Nth(basis, r), 0), e, 1),
                      UpdateBinv(binv, d, r), iter + 1, du)

Rem ---- building the dual, one sparse column at a time ------------------
Rem Column j of the dual, for j a primal constraint, is primal row j
Rem with each entry scaled by its own row's flip - which is why the
Rem primal is transposed once, here, and never again.

Def FlipVs(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, flips As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, Length(ix)), Fn(k) => Nth(vs, k) * Nth(flips, Nth(ix, k))))

Rem ---- the public entries: primal in, full result out ------------------

Def SpOptimize(p As SpProblem) As Solution
    Let n = Length(Costs(p))
    Let m = Length(Rhs(p))
    If Rows(SA(p)) <> m Or Cols(SA(p)) <> n Then
        Error("SIMPLEX: MATRIX DOES NOT MATCH ITS COSTS AND RHS")
    Else
        Let ta = SpTransp(SA(p))
        Rem Rows whose rhs (a primal cost) is negative are negated for a
        Rem feasible start and get a big-M artificial; a clean problem gets
        Rem none and computes exactly as the classic slack start.
        Let flips = ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => SignFor(Nth(Costs(p), i))))
        Let arts = Filter(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Nth(flips, i) < 0)
        Let bigM = 1000 * (1 + MaxAbs(Rhs(p)))
        Rem The dual's columns: one per primal constraint, then one
        Rem single-entry slack per dual row, then one single-entry
        Rem artificial per flipped row. No identity is ever built.
        Let ix = Concat(Concat(Map(Range(1, m), Fn(j) => SpColIx(ta, j)),
                               Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Dim(1, i))),
                        Map(arts, Fn(i) => Dim(1, i)))
        Let vs = Concat(Concat(Map(Range(1, m), Fn(j) => FlipVs(SpColIx(ta, j), SpColVs(ta, j), flips)),
                               Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Dim(1, Nth(flips, i)))),
                        Map(arts, Fn(i) => Dim(1, 1)))
        Let dco = Concat(Concat(Rhs(p), Dim(n, 0)), Dim(Length(arts), 0 - bigM))
        Let drh = ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Nth(Costs(p), i) * Nth(flips, i)))
        Let du = Dual(SpFromCols(n, ix, vs), dco, drh, m, flips)
        Let basis = ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => BasisSlot(i, flips, m, n, arts)))
        Solve(basis, BasisFlags(basis, Length(dco)), Ident(n), 1, du)

Def Optimize(p As Problem) As Solution
    SpOptimize(SpProblem(Costs(p), SpFromMat(A(p)), Rhs(p)))
